<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grand Trunk Road</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Grand Trunk Road is one of the oldest and longest roads in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, running about 2,500 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. An ancient route, it was rebuilt in the 1540s by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a>, who lined it with trees, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a> and posting stations, and was improved by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and the British. It remains a major highway.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grand-trunk-road.jpg" alt="Grand Trunk Road" width="320" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas(CA2) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand_Trunk_Road_Route.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
